        <TopPick />

        <div id="daily" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Daily Routine: Wipe, Rinse, Dry
          </h2>

          <p>
            Day to day, marble needs less than most people give it. Wipe crumbs and debris off first with a dry soft cloth or brush, so you are not dragging grit across the surface. Then wipe the counter with a soft cloth or microfiber dampened with warm water and a pH-neutral stone cleaner. That lifts everyday grease and fingerprints without touching the stone or the sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rinse if your cleaner calls for it, then dry the counter with a clean, dry microfiber cloth. Drying is the step most people skip, and it matters on marble. Water left to evaporate leaves mineral spots, especially in hard-water areas, and water pooling around a faucet day after day slowly wears at the sealer and the stone around the sink.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use separate cloths for washing and drying, and wash them often. A cloth that has been used on the stove or the sink drags grease and grit across the marble, and microfiber holds onto both. Many owners keep a dedicated set of cloths just for the stone, which makes the routine quicker and avoids cross-contamination with harsher kitchen cleaners.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the whole daily routine. It takes a minute or two, and it does more to keep marble looking new than any special product. The pillar guide to <a href="/best-marble-sealer/">the best marble sealers</a> includes a similar daily, monthly and yearly schedule, and the daily part is always the same: neutral cleaner, soft cloth, dry as you go.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What to Use: pH-Neutral Cleaners and Mild Soap
          </h2>

          <p>
            The safest cleaners for marble are pH-neutral, meaning neither acidic nor strongly alkaline. Acids react with the calcium carbonate in marble and etch it. Strong alkalis, such as bleach and many degreasers, do not etch the stone in the same way, but they break down sealer over time. A neutral product cleans without doing either.
          </p>

          <p>
            Purpose-made stone cleaners are labeled as safe for marble and natural stone and are usually sold as sprays. Many brands that make stone sealers, including several on our <a href="/best-marble-sealer/">marble sealer list</a>, also make a daily stone cleaner designed to work with their sealers. Check the label for wording such as pH-neutral or safe for marble, not just safe for granite, since marble is more sensitive.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you prefer not to buy a dedicated product, warm water with a few drops of a mild, unscented dish soap is a common recommendation in natural stone care guidance. Use very little soap, because too much leaves a film that dulls the surface and attracts dirt. Rinse with clean water and dry. Avoid dish soaps with added citrus, which may be acidic, or with abrasive particles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plain warm water on a microfiber cloth also works for light everyday cleaning, and it cannot harm the stone or the sealer. Many marble owners use water alone most days and a stone cleaner once or twice a week. If the stone is well sealed, as covered in our guide to <a href="/how-to-seal-marble/">sealing marble</a>, most spills and grease wipe up with very little effort.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="avoid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Never to Use on Marble
          </h2>

          <p>
            The list of things to avoid is longer than the list of things to use, and many of them are sitting under the average kitchen sink. Acidic products etch marble on contact, leaving dull patches. Harsh alkaline products strip sealer, which makes the stone more prone to staining. Abrasives scratch polished stone. All three create damage that has to be repaired rather than cleaned.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Avoid</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use instead</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vinegar and vinegar-based sprays</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acidic; etches marble</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">pH-neutral stone cleaner</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lemon, lime, citrus cleaners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acidic; etches marble</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unscented mild dish soap in water</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bleach and bleach sprays</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strongly alkaline; breaks down sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stone-safe cleaner; see disinfecting below</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ammonia and glass cleaners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Alkaline; dulls and strips sealer with regular use</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Microfiber dampened with water</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Most disinfecting wipes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Many contain citric acid or harsh chemicals</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stone-safe disinfecting approach</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bathroom and tub-and-tile cleaners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often acidic or abrasive</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">pH-neutral stone cleaner</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Scouring powders and creams</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Abrasive; scratches and dulls</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soft cloth, plastic scraper for stuck food</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Abrasive pads and melamine sponges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fine abrasive; dulls polish</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Microfiber cloth</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The marble-specific nature of this list is worth stressing. Some cleaners labeled safe for granite are fine on granite but not on marble, because granite is not calcium-based and does not etch the same way. Read labels for the word marble, or for limestone and travertine, which share the same chemistry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Homemade cleaning recipes are a particular trap. Many popular DIY sprays combine vinegar, lemon juice or citric acid with dish soap, which is great for some surfaces and genuinely damaging for marble. If a recipe includes anything sour or acidic, do not use it on marble, and keep the bottle out of reach of anyone else who cleans the kitchen.
          </p>

          <p>
            If an acidic cleaner has already been used regularly, the marble may look uniformly dull or have hazy patches around the sink and cooktop. That is etching, and it can usually be polished out on polished marble, as described in our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-etching-from-marble/">removing etching from marble</a>. Switching cleaners stops the damage from coming back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weekly" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Weekly Deep Clean and Disinfecting
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once a week, clear the counter completely and clean it end to end, including behind appliances and under the fruit bowl, where grease and crumbs collect. Use the same pH-neutral cleaner, perhaps a little more of it, and a soft cloth. For stuck-on food, let a damp cloth sit on it for a minute to soften it, then lift it with a plastic scraper rather than scrubbing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Disinfecting marble without damaging it takes some care. Bleach, many disinfecting sprays and most disinfecting wipes are either acidic or harsh enough to strip sealer. Some stone cleaners are formulated to disinfect as well as clean and say so on the label; those are the simplest option. Hot water and a neutral cleaner remove most germs physically, which is enough for everyday kitchen use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some stone care guidance suggests that a solution of isopropyl alcohol and water, often around a 50/50 mix, can be used occasionally to disinfect sealed marble. Alcohol is not acidic, but repeated, heavy use may affect some sealers, so treat it as an occasional measure rather than the daily cleaner, and check the guidance from your sealer's manufacturer. Rinse and dry afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the weekly clean to check on the stone. Look at the counter at a low angle for dull patches that might be new etches, check for any colored marks that could become stains, and notice whether water still beads near the sink. If water is soaking in quickly there, it is time to test properly and reseal, as covered in <a href="/how-often-to-seal-marble/">how often to seal marble</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spills" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Handling Spills as They Happen
          </h2>

          <p>
            Speed matters more on marble than on almost any other countertop. Acidic spills such as citrus juice, wine, vinegar, tomato sauce and soda start etching within seconds to minutes, regardless of sealer. Blot them up immediately with a damp cloth, rinse the spot with water, and dry it. Do not wipe in wide circles, which spreads the spill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oil-based spills such as cooking oil, butter and salad dressing do not etch but can stain if left. A good sealer gives you time, often minutes to hours, before oil starts to soak in. Blot the spill, then clean the spot with a little neutral cleaner or mild soap and water to remove the remaining film, and dry it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Colored spills such as coffee, tea, wine, berries and turmeric can stain as well as, or instead of, etching. Blot and rinse promptly. If a mark remains after cleaning and drying, it may be a stain that needs a poultice, described in our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-stains-from-marble/">removing stains from marble</a>. Act within a day or two, while it is still near the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a cloth and a spray of neutral cleaner within easy reach of the marble so cleaning up is quicker than ignoring it. In busy kitchens that is the single most useful habit. Most marble damage comes not from one dramatic spill but from small ones left for an hour while dinner happens.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="film" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Hard Water, Soap Film and Dullness
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dull, cloudy look on marble around a faucet usually has one of three causes: hard-water mineral deposits, soap or cleaner film, or etching. Each needs a different response. Mineral deposits and film sit on the surface and can be removed; etching is damage to the surface and has to be polished out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Soap film builds up when too much dish soap is used or the counter is not rinsed. It shows as streaks or a hazy surface that feels slightly tacky. Wash the area with plain warm water and a clean microfiber, rinse well, and dry. Switching to a dedicated stone cleaner, which is formulated not to leave residue, usually stops it returning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hard-water deposits are white, chalky crusts or rings, often around faucets. Do not use descalers, which are acidic and etch the stone. Natural Stone Institute guidance suggests buffing light mineral deposits with dry, very fine 0000 steel wool; test it on a hidden area first on polished marble. A plastic scraper can lift thicker crusts. Drying the faucet area daily prevents them.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the dullness is even across the busy parts of the counter and does not respond to cleaning, it is probably etching from years of acidic cleaners or spills. A stone restoration professional can hone and repolish the surface, after which the stone should be resealed and cleaned only with neutral products from then on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="habits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Habits That Keep Marble Looking New
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use cutting boards, trivets and coasters. Cutting directly on marble dulls knives and can scratch polished stone. Hot pans can affect sealer and, in extreme cases, crack stone through thermal shock. Glasses of wine, juice and cocktails leave etched rings. A few boards and coasters prevent most everyday damage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put oils, vinegars and toiletries on trays. Bottles of olive oil, balsamic vinegar and soy sauce left on the counter drip down their sides and leave rings, and so do perfumes and mouthwash on bathroom vanities. A small tray catches the drips and is far easier to clean than the stone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the stone sealed. Sealer does not stop etching, but it gives you time to wipe up oil and colored spills before they stain, and it makes routine cleaning easier because dirt sits on the surface rather than in the pores. Test the busy areas every few months and reseal as needed, using a product from our <a href="/best-marble-sealer/">best marble sealers</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, tell everyone who cleans the kitchen. Many marble countertops are etched by a well-meaning family member, guest or cleaner with a vinegar spray or a bleach wipe. A small note inside the cabinet with the right cleaner, or simply keeping only stone-safe products under the sink, prevents most of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using vinegar or lemon cleaners</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Vinegar and citrus are popular natural cleaners and they etch marble on contact, leaving dull patches that need polishing. Keep anything acidic off marble entirely, including homemade sprays and citrus-scented products.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Disinfecting with bleach or wipes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bleach and many disinfecting wipes are harsh enough to break down sealer, and some wipes contain citric acid that etches. Use a stone-safe disinfecting cleaner instead, or follow your sealer maker's guidance for occasional disinfection.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using too much dish soap</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A few drops of mild soap is enough. More than that leaves a film that dulls the surface and attracts dirt. If the counter looks streaky or cloudy, rinse with plain water and switch to a dedicated stone cleaner.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting water air-dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water left on marble leaves mineral spots, and standing water around faucets wears at the sealer. Drying the counter with a microfiber cloth after cleaning takes seconds and prevents most hard-water marks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Scrubbing with abrasives</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Scouring powders, abrasive pads and melamine sponges remove the polish along with the dirt. Soften stuck food with a damp cloth and lift it with a plastic scraper instead, and keep abrasives away from polished marble.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best thing to clean marble countertops with?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A pH-neutral cleaner made for natural stone, or warm water with a few drops of mild, unscented dish soap, applied with a soft microfiber cloth. Rinse if needed and dry the counter afterwards. Avoid anything acidic, strongly alkaline or abrasive.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use Windex on marble?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is best avoided. Many glass cleaners contain ammonia or other chemicals that can dull marble and break down sealer with regular use, and some versions contain vinegar. A microfiber cloth dampened with water, or a stone-safe cleaner, cleans marble just as well.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is dish soap safe for marble?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A few drops of mild, unscented dish soap in warm water is generally considered safe for sealed marble. Use very little and rinse afterwards, because soap residue leaves a dull film. Avoid soaps with citrus additives or abrasive particles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I disinfect marble countertops?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a cleaner labeled as both disinfecting and safe for marble, which is the simplest option. Some stone care sources suggest an occasional wipe with diluted isopropyl alcohol on sealed marble; check your sealer maker's guidance. Avoid bleach and most disinfecting wipes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Clorox or Lysol wipes safe on marble?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most disinfecting wipes are not recommended for marble, because many contain citric acid or other ingredients that etch the stone or wear out the sealer. Check the label carefully, and if it does not say safe for marble, use a stone-safe cleaner instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I get dull spots off marble countertops?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                First work out what they are. Soap film and hard-water deposits sit on the surface and can be cleaned off with water, a stone cleaner or gentle buffing. Dull spots that do not clean away are etches from acid and need polishing with a marble polishing compound.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I clean marble countertops?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wipe them daily with water or a neutral cleaner and dry them, clean the whole surface more thoroughly once a week, and wipe spills immediately. Test the sealer every few months and reseal the busy areas when water starts soaking in.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

